      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Let $a=96$ and $n=7.$ Find positive integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a=qn+r,$ where $0\\le r<n,$ and determine $q\\cdot r.$",
      "worked_solution": "Dividing $96$ by $7$ using integer division, we notice that\n\n$$\n13<96÷7<14.\n$$\n\nThe quotient $q$ is the left-hand value. So $q=13.$\n\nNext, we find the remainder $r$ by computing $r=a-qn.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=96-(13)(7)=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write $96=qn+r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}96={{13}_{⏟}}_{q}\\cdot 7+{{5}_{⏟}}_{r}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $q\\cdot r=13\\cdot 5=65.$",
